del diluvio","book":"A History of Rock Music 1951-2000","author":"Piero Scaruffi","start":1957,"end":1962,"desc":"La «edad oscura» del rock'n'roll: los rebeldes son sustituidos por ídolos adolescentes educados y el Brill Building devuelve el poder a los compositores profesionales. Pero en esos años se incuban la revolución futura: la guitarra fuzz de Link Wray, los bailes de moda (el twist), el rock instrumental de guitarras (Duane Eddy, Ventures, Shadows), el calipso y el primer ska, el doo-wop más inventivo (Coasters, Drifters, Five Royales), el nacimiento del soul (Sam Cooke, Ray Charles, Motown, Stax), el estudio como instrumento de Joe Meek y el surf de los Beach Boys y del «muro de sonido» de Phil Spector.","color":"#f59e0b","family":"Raíces: rock'n'roll, country y roots","link":"https://volteret4.github.io/scaruffi/#before-flood"}],"book_tracks":[{"id":101700517981966,"artist":"Harry Belafonte","artist_id":15786213965391,"title":"Banana Boat","year":null,"ess":0,"yt":"H5dpBWlRANE","kind":"s","book":{"id":8,"title":"A History of Rock Music 1951-2000","author":"Piero Scaruffi","url":"https://volteret4.github.io/scaruffi/"},"scene":"Antes del diluvio","scene_id":169050477627223,"scene_link":"https://volteret4.github.io/scaruffi/#before-flood","chapters":["Sección 2.3"],"pages":[[1,18,null]]}],"mentions":[{"book":"A History of Rock Music 1951-2000","author":"Piero Scaruffi","page":18,"text":"…Rock (1959). Exotica Exotic sounds were still percolating into the American psyche, but now the emphasis was shifting towards Latin America. In 1957 Harry Belafonte's Banana Boat launched \"calypso\", and in 1959 the Drifters' There Goes My Baby introduced Latin rhythm to pop music. In the meantime, a Jamaican…"},{"book":"A History of Rock Music 1951-2000","author":"Piero Scaruffi","page":311,"text":"…guitar sound 1957 ？ LaMonte Young composes music for sustained tones ？ Max Mathews begins composing computer music at Bell Laboratories ？ Harry Belafonte's Banana Boat launches \"calypso\" 311"}],"podcast":[],"awards":[{"kind":"award","title":"Theatre World Award (1954)","body":"Won.","year":1954,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"Wikidata","episode":null,"url":"https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q1131356"}]},{"kind":"award","title":"Tony Award for Best Featured Actor in a Musical (1954)","body":"Won.","year":1954,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"Wikidata","episode":null,"url":"https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q674165"}]},{"kind":"award","title":"Emmy Award (1960)","body":"Won.","year":1960,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"Wikidata","episode":null,"url":"https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q123737"}]},{"kind":"award","title":"Paul Robeson Award (1979)","body":"Won.","year":1979,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"Wikidata","episode":null,"url":"https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q7153268"}]},{"kind":"award","title":"Kennedy Center Honors (1989)","body":"Won.","year":1989,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"Wikidata","episode":null,"url":"https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q1738793"}]},{"kind":"award","title":"honorary degree from Spelman College (1990)","body":"Won.","year":1990,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"Wikidata","episode":null,"url":"https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q121364143"}]},{"kind":"award","title":"National Medal of Arts (1994)","body":"Won.","year":1994,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"Wikidata","episode":null,"url":"https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q1789030"}]},{"kind":"award","title":"Marian Anderson Award (1998)","body":"Won.","year":1998,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"Wikidata","episode":null,"url":"https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q6761828"}]},{"kind":"award","title":"Grammy Lifetime Achievement Award (2000)","body":"Won.","year":2000,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"Wikidata","episode":null,"url":"https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q935843"}]},{"kind":"award","title":"Bishop John T. Walker Distinguished Humanitarian Service Award (2002)","body":"Won.","year":2002,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"Wikidata","episode":null,"url":"https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q4917609"}]}